\begin_layout Subsubsection
Nearest neighbor
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I used python to apply 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

-Nearest neighbor (numpy and scikit-learn).
 I used the euclidean norm as the distance metric with no regularization.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I used cross-validation with five validation sets to determine the number of neighbors 
\begin_inset Formula $(K)$
\end_inset

.
 I used the mean 0-1 loss of the validation sets for each 
\begin_inset Formula $K\in\{1,2,\cdots,100\}$
\end_inset

 as measure of the predictive performance of 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

.
 Using the method i found,
 that 
\begin_inset Formula $K=60$
\end_inset

 gave the minimum mean 0-1 loss with 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{validation}\approx0.1494$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using 
\begin_inset Formula $K=60$
\end_inset

 i calculated the training 0-1 loss:
 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{train}\approx0.1462$
\end_inset

,
 and the test 0-1 loss:
 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{test}\approx0.0980$
\end_inset

.
 Surprisingly the test 0-1 loss was the lowest of all the 0-1 losses by quiet a lot.
\end_layout

